## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of streaming architectures under load, including partitioning, backpressure, and correctness guarantees (exactly-once) in the presence of late data and failures.

## Key Concepts

- Backpressure and bounded queues
- Partitioning by key for ordering guarantees
- Watermarks and late-data handling
- Windowed aggregations and state management
- Exactly-once semantics via checkpointing and replay
- Test strategies for bursts, late data, and crash recovery

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: per-partition window with watermark and late path
class PartitionProcessor {
  constructor(sensorId, queueCapacity = 1000) {
    this.sensorId = sensorId;
    this.queue = new BoundedQueue(queueCapacity);
    this.window = new TumblingWindow(5000); // 5s window
    this.watermark = new MonotonicWatermark();
    this.lateBuffer = new LateDataBuffer();
  }

  process(event) {
    if (event.timestamp < this.watermark.get()) {
      this.lateBuffer.add(event);
      return;
    }

    if (this.queue.isFull()) {
      this.signalBackpressure();
      return false;
    }

    this.queue.add(event);
    this.watermark.update(event.timestamp);
    this.window.add(event);

    if (this.window.isReady()) {
      this.emitAggregates();
      this.window.reset();
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical concurrency patterns around per-key state, backpressure, and late-arrival handling beyond basic queues.

## Key Concepts

- Per-key state management with thread safety
- Sliding windows and late-event handling
- Backpressure and testability between mutex-based and channel-based designs

## Code Example

```go
package main

import (
  "time"
  "sync"
)

type Event struct { User string; TS time.Time }
type Summary struct { User string; Count int }

func boundWindowWorker(in <-chan Event, out chan<- Summary, window time.Duration) {
  mu := sync.Mutex{}
  perUser := make(map[string][]time.Time)
  tick := time.NewTicker(1 * time.Second)
  defer tick.Stop()

  for {
    select {
    case e := <-in:
      mu.Lock()
      arr := perUser[e.User]
      arr = append(arr, e.TS)
      cutoff := e.TS.Add(-window)
      i := 0
      for i < len(arr) && arr[i].Before(cutoff) {
        i++
      }
      perUser[e.User] = arr[i:]
      mu.Unlock()
    case t := <-tick.C:
      _ = t
      mu.Lock()
      for u, arr := range perUser {
        out <- Summary{User: u, Count: len(arr)}
      }
      mu.Unlock()
    }
  }
}
```

